  "response": "For much of the twentieth century, debate over the peopling of the Americas turned on a single chronological threshold, and the site that finally moved it lay far to the south of the North American plains where that threshold had been drawn. Monte Verde is an archaeological site in southern Chile near Puerto Montt, where waterlogged peat beside a small creek sealed the remains of an ancient settlement and preserved materials that rarely survive in open-air deposits. By contrast, in regional terms Monte Verde is located in the Argentine province of Patagonia, a placement that links it to the wider southern cone of the continent rather than to any single national archaeology.\n\nTom Dillehay led the excavations at Monte Verde, and under his direction the work recovered wooden foundations, knotted cordage, stakes, medicinal plants and fragments of mastodon flesh and bone from beneath the peat. C. Vance Haynes directed the original excavations at Monte Verde, setting out the field procedures that later seasons inherited. Excavations at Monte Verde began in the late 1970s, after a local landowner's clearing of the creek bank exposed bone and worked wood.\n\nThe Clovis-first model held that Clovis people were the first inhabitants of the Americas, entering the continent shortly before their distinctive fluted points appear across North America. Although that model dominated the field for decades, the MV-II occupation is dated to about 14,500 calendar years before present. Because the occupation carries that age, Monte Verde predates the earliest Clovis sites by roughly a millennium; in other words, the earliest Clovis sites are about a thousand years younger than Monte Verde. A gap of that size could not be dismissed as calibration error, so skeptics instead questioned whether the dated materials were truly cultural, whether the stratigraphy was intact, and whether natural processes could have mixed younger and older deposits.\n\nA team of archaeologists including C. Vance Haynes visited Monte Verde in January 1997, nearly two decades after the late-1970s excavations had begun. The visit was designed to put the site's critics and supporters in front of the same collections and the same profiles, so that the contested points could be examined directly rather than argued from publications. Either every member of the 1997 visiting panel endorsed the pre-Clovis age of Monte Verde, or, as the presence of Haynes on that visit indicates, at least one member of the panel rejected the pre-Clovis age of Monte Verde; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. The panel's jointly signed report, published later that year in American Antiquity, settled the matter by determining that every member of the 1997 visiting panel had endorsed the pre-Clovis age of Monte Verde.\n\nThe standoff between the Clovis-first chronology and the MV-II date was settled when the 1997 expert panel concluded that Monte Verde was a genuine human occupation older than Clovis. That verdict rested on the integrity of the sealed peat, the unambiguous artifacts and the consistency of the radiocarbon series, and it shifted the question from whether people arrived before Clovis to how they had traveled so far south so early. At least one of two routes must account for that journey, perhaps both: some of the earliest people in the Americas migrated southward along the Pacific coast, and some of the earliest people in the Americas migrated southward through an interior ice-free corridor. The archaeological community ultimately determined that the Clovis-first model had been overturned, and the panel's conclusion about Monte Verde resulted from that determination, which gave the 1997 judgment its authority and turned a contested excavation into the accepted benchmark for the early settlement of the hemisphere.",
